You need to build the kernel via "make boot", and then find the stripped and
gzipped kernel as arch/alpha/boot/vmlinux.gz. Doing it by hand will result
in an unbootable kernel if not stripped correctly. Depend on the "make boot"
for this.

> Other than that I'm not sure what else is worth mentioning. I've been
> having a lot of stability problems with the 2.0.18 kernel. I'll be
> building away and it will hang. It'll be linking vmlinux and claim Oops
> couldn't page in virtual address xxxx. Lots of weird behavior. I hope
> there isn't some hardware problem making this whole thing more difficult.

I'd try out the 2.0.27 kernel plus axp-diffs (the 2.0.21 ones should work),
as it's the latest and greatest, and may be better, stability-wise. And yes,
the FDDI driver was indeed tested out (by me) on the Alphas back around
2.0.21, IIRC, but I've not tried it since.

