Subject: Re: Confusion re SRM, ARC, MILO, etc.
In-Reply-To: <Pine.SOL.3.95.961213144626.24776D-100000@shell1.aimnet.com>

On Fri, 13 Dec 1996, Terry Carroll wrote:

> Can someone please give me the quick tour of what SRM, ARC and MILO are,
> and the distinctions between them?
> 
> Here's my (mis)conceptions so far; corrections are solicited:
> 
>  1. SRM is the default console;

Usually on the UDB, arc is the default.  SRM is the Digital Unix console.  

>  2. ARC is another alternate console, but the documentation I've seen
> leads me to believe that it's ordinarily used only with Windows NT, and
> isn't applicable to Linux.

ARC is indeed the Windows NT boot console. Tradiditional blue background.
This is usually the default on UDB... and _encouraged_ for booting linux.
(it is _far_ easier to set up linload.exe and milo on this)

>  3. MILO is a loader comparable to LILO, but that also loads some sort of
> microcode.  It runs in conjunction with either SRM or ARC, but not in
> place of them.

Right.  ARC and SRM are usually in the Flash rom... and Milo usually
resides on a small MS-DOS partition (in the ARC case).  Though milo _can_
be put in flash rom, too.

>  4. SRM or ARC is an either-or thing: you run either SRM or ARC, but not
> both (can you even install both?).

My UDB came with both installed.

